  "result": "I wrote `tests/unit/intervals.test.ts` with 15 tests, and all of them pass under `npx vitest run`. I didn't touch `src/intervals.ts`.\n\nThe tests cover:\n- **Edge cases:** an empty list, a single block, a block that starts and ends at the same point, duplicate blocks, and negative numbers.\n- **Merging:** blocks that overlap, blocks that share an endpoint, one block inside another, and a chain of blocks given out of order.\n- **Ordering:** unsorted input comes back sorted, and blocks with a real gap between them stay separate.\n- **Safety:** the input isn't changed, and the result contains new arrays rather than the ones passed in.\n- **Overall check:** in a mixed input, the result is sorted and no two blocks overlap.\n\n**Decision for you:** the code treats blocks as touching when the next one starts within 1 of the previous end (`start <= last[1] + 1` on line 17). So `[1,2]` and `[3,4]` merge into `[1,4]`, which makes sense if blocks are whole-number slots. But `[1,2]` and `[2.5,3]` also merge, even though they neither overlap nor touch as the comment describes it. Because the intended behaviour isn't clear, I left this case untested. If you tell me whether blocks are always whole numbers, I'll add a test that pins it down; if fractional values are allowed, that test would fail and show the merge as a bug.",
